count limits too.\n\nNow I'm constructing the test input files. For the -A flag, I need to cover tabs, control characters, and line endings. I'll use a test string with tabs that display as ^I, control characters like 0x01 and 0x02 that show as ^A and ^B, line endings marked with $, and multiple blank lines to test the squeeze behavior.\n\nFor -e, the same file works but tabs pass through since -T isn't enabled. For -t, tabs display as ^I and control chars as ^X without the $ markers. The -u flag should produce identical output to no flags.\n\nWriting the YAML test definition... Now I'm setting up differential tests for the flag aliases, comparing our cat implementation against GNU cat for the -A, -e, and -t flags to ensure they produce identical output when handling control characters, tabs, and newlines. I'll follow the existing test patterns from the cat test file, using parallel execution, temporary directories, and the standard diff test framework. My test inputs will mix control characters, tabs, high bytes, and blank lines to exercise all the display paths that these flag aliases activate. I'm keeping each test focused on a single flag alias, with the combined -A -n test as its own function to avoid cluttering the individual tests.\n\nNow I need to verify that each flag alias produces output matching gcat exactly: -A for all non-printing characters, -e for line endings and non-printing characters, -t for tabs and non-printing characters, -u for unbuffered output, and the -A -n combination together. I'm verifying the task against all the constraints one more time—checking tool usage, filesystem access, code size, requirement counts, and release scope. Everything checks out, so I'm ready to finalize this.\n\nActually, I should double-check the existing test file header to see what requirements are already documented. The current header lists several requirements, but my new task adds R4.5 through R4.8, so I need to make sure the test file header gets updated to